You want something from, or tell something to, a repo at your own level: client ↔ client, server ↔ server, microservice ↔ microservice, in your project or another product.

- Answering a peer → `sdh-peer-channel-answer`. Asking the level above or below → `sdh-cross-repo-req-send`. Pushing down or up → `sdh-cross-repo-announce-send`.
- Equals only. A client never opens an exchange with a server — not even another product's: it asks its own server through REQ.
- **A channel exists only where the owner declared it.** Your counterparts are listed in `docs/project.md`, `## Peer channels`. Not listed → no channel: ask the owner to link the pair; never create folders by hand.
- Equals owe each other nothing: unlike REQ, `declined` is a normal answer.

## Folders

| Folder | Holds | Who writes |
|---|---|---|
| `docs/InOutBox/outbox/<peer>/` | what you sent | you |
| `docs/InOutBox/inbox/<peer>/` | what the peer sent | the app only |
| `docs/InOutBox/pact/<peer>/` | agreements | both, one file per writer |

`<peer>` is the last segment of the peer's repo name. You write only in your own repo — never in `inbox/`, never in another repo. The app mirrors your `outbox/<peer>/` into their `inbox/<you>/` and merges the `pact/` folders.

## Kind

| Kind | What | Answer | Folder |
|---|---|---|---|
| `PACT-NNN_<slug>.md` | a two-sided agreement | `.ack.md` required | `pact/<peer>/` |
| `HANDOFF-NNN_<slug>.md` | you hand an area over | none | `outbox/<peer>/` |
| `NOTE-NNN_<slug>.md` | an observation or question | none (a reply NOTE is fine) | `outbox/<peer>/` |

Does the peer have to commit to something? Yes → PACT, no → NOTE. There is no "request" form.

## Number — from `docs/InOutBox/registry/<peer>.md`

- `max` over every line of every `docs/InOutBox/registry/*.md` plus the
  frozen legacy ledgers + 1 — one counter per repo, shared across kinds and
  directions, incoming numbers included, across all counterparties. Never
  from the folder, never reused.
- Line into `registry/<peer>.md`:
  `- PACT-003 | out | LenSa_Assist1C | shared code: console is the donor | 2026-08-24 | | |`
  — `id | direction | counterpart | subject | opened | closed | final status |
  where the result went`. Empty `closed` = in flight.
- Write the line in the same pass as the file; the app never writes or
  deletes the registry. First touch: split a flat `docs/InOutBox/registry.md`
  and frozen `docs/*-requirements/**/registry.md` ledgers into per-pair files
  by counterpart; originals stay byte-identical. A row whose counterpart does
  not resolve stays archived — raise it; the number still counts.
- In a shared repo the number is issued on a current `dev`: sync, write the
  ledger line, then publish the body — parallel `max+1` without checking is
  a collision.

## PACT body

```markdown
# PACT-NNN: <the point>

**Parties:** <initiator> ↔ <counterpart>
**Authority on this subject:** <repo> — and why

## Subject
## Obligations
- <repo A>: …
- <repo B>: …
## How it is verified
## Out of scope
```

The authority line is mandatory and names the authority for this subject; the same pair can hold opposite roles in two pacts.

One file, one writer: the initiator writes `PACT-NNN.md` and later `.impl.md`, the peer writes `.ack.md`. Never edit the peer's file.

## Closing

- PACT → the initiator drops `PACT-NNN_<slug>.impl.md`; HANDOFF / NOTE → the sender drops `<name>.done.md` beside the body. The app removes the files on both sides.
- Drop the marker only after the peer has had a sync to read the body. Signs it was read: their `.done.md` on the exchange that prompted yours, a reply NOTE, or a task in their plan; none → close at your next release's inbox scan and say so in the registry.
- Fill `closed`, final status and where the result went in the same pass.

## Never

- Agree between equals about what the level above owns: each side sends its own REQ up; a pact may only record who sends it.
- Use the channel as a task list: the work lives in each side's `docs/todo.md`.
